     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · TechCrunch · 2026-11-02 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Anthropic Warns of Imminent &quot;Recursive Self-Improvement&quot; in AI Capabilities</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#AI</span> <span>#Anthropic</span> <span>#RSI</span> <span>#Technology</span></div>
       <p>Anthropic has signaled it is nearing the capability for Recursive Self-Improvement (RSI), a concept also referred to as self-building AI. This significant disclosure emerged within the fine print of the company's revised S-1A filing on Monday, a document that also revealed the AI lab's updated financials showing both soaring revenue and escalating expenses. RSI is precisely defined as the critical juncture at which artificial intelligence becomes so adept at training itself that it triggers a dramatic and accelerated advancement of its own capabilities. For those concerned about the future of AI, often termed &quot;AI doomers,&quot; RSI represents a potential cataclysm from which there may be no return, envisioning AI developing into a power beyond human control, potentially indifferent to the damage it might cause while pursuing its objectives. Conversely, AI enthusiasts view RSI, or a similar reliable self-improvement mechanism, as the ultimate promise of the technology, foreseeing it as a tool for unimaginable productivity, scientific breakthroughs, and more.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/anthropic-warns-of-imminent-recursive-self-improvement-in-ai.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The company stated explicitly in its S-1 filing that it already incorporates AI into its research and development processes, yet it issued a clear caution regarding this practice. Anthropic noted, &quot;Using AI to help build more capable AI could also pose a considerable safety risk, particularly if it sharply accelerates the pace of progress.&quot; This specific concern is a central component of their Responsible Scaling Policy, which outlines the company's strategies to monitor and actively mitigate these risks as it approaches the threshold of 'recursive self-improvement.' Earlier this year, the term RSI was often dismissed by many as mere buzzword or a goal deemed too challenging to achieve, define, or prove.</p><p>However, the new statements added to Anthropic's Risk Factors section on Monday provided a more urgent perspective. The filing disclosed that the company has, in the past, used AI to develop new models whose significant leaps in capability were not identified at the time they occurred. Crucially, the document indicated that Anthropic believes it may soon meet its own internal definition of RSI. The S-1 stated with emphasis, &quot;our internal estimates and our expectations regarding future models, rather than our current models, indicate that we may imminently meet our Responsible Scaling Policy’s definition of 'dramatically accelerated progress attributable to AI.'&quot; The company, however, added that it has not designated any of its current models as exceeding this threshold and is still assessing the underlying data.</p><p>Anthropic also affirmed in the filing that none of its existing models have yet crossed the RSI threshold, and it is committed to maintaining human involvement in the R&amp;D process for the foreseeable future. &quot;We do not anticipate being able to safely use Claude to fully autonomously design and build successive generations of frontier models without meaningful human supervision or understanding, if and when that becomes technically possible,&quot; the company stated. Accordingly, Anthropic is considering updates to its Responsible Scaling Policy to ensure that humans remain in control of frontier model development. While some skeptics might suggest that an S-1 filing serves both as a disclosure and a marketing tool, and that claims of nearing such a powerful model could fall into the latter category, there is no denying that Claude’s capabilities, particularly in areas like coding, mathematics, and cybersecurity, have demonstrated &quot;leaps and bounds&quot; of growth this year. The source article suggests that if Anthropic perceives the possibility of RSI as close, it may warrant a belief that calls for appropriate action and governance.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the S-1A filing details, including the specific risks and Anthropic's stated position on RSI.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://techcrunch.com/2026/11/02/anthropic-warns-that-its-very-close-to-rsi-capability-aka-self-building-ai/" rel="noopener">TechCrunch</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
