     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Deep-Sea Mining Leaves Lasting Impact on Seafloor Ecosystems</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers from the University of Liverpool studied a 1979 deep-sea mining test site in the Pacific Ocean's Clarion-Clipperton Zone. Their analysis revealed that 44 years after the disturbance, the seafloor still suffers from depleted quality and quantity of particulate organic matter, which serves as a vital nutrient source for seafloor organisms.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/deep-sea-mining-leaves-lasting-impact-on-seafloor-ecosystems.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study linked this lack of organic matter to lower abundances of both large animals and single-celled organisms in the area. These findings highlight that deep-sea mining causes long-term ecological damage, potentially hindering the recovery and resilience of seafloor ecosystems against the industrial extraction of rare metals.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the study's findings regarding the long-term ecological impact of deep-sea mining on organic matter and organism abundance.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-impact-deep-sea-seafloor-ecosystem.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
