     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-11-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Google Severs Ties With Manufacturer After Botnet Discovery</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Google has dismantled a botnet embedded within 1.3 million Android devices by manufacturing partner Videostrong Technology Co. The Shenzhen-based firm allegedly installed a hidden backdoor to facilitate ad fraud, data exfiltration, and unauthorized network traffic for financial gain. Google has banned the company from producing or supplying software for certified Android devices.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/google-severs-ties-with-manufacturer-after-botnet-discovery.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The malware targeted various Android TV boxes, telehealth equipment, and digital signage. Google utilized obfuscation techniques to uncover the threat and has since revised its manufacturer review processes to prevent future compromises. Disrupting the associated command and control domains is expected to mitigate access to an additional 10 million compromised devices globally.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise summary of the botnet discovery and Google's response.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/1024807/google-botnet-android-tv-shut-down" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
