     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-11-02 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>ICC Men's Cricket World Cup 2027: Over 120 Million Ticket Requests Received</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#World Cup 2027</span> <span>#ICC</span> <span>#Ticket Sales</span></div>
       <p>Global demand for the ICC Men’s Cricket World Cup 2027 has surged, with total ticket requests surpassing 120 million, reaching 120,809,467 applications as of November 1. This overwhelming response, according to Crictracker, includes 2.8 million unique fans from 193 countries who registered for the opportunity to purchase tickets for the 14th edition of ODI cricket’s premier event. With the ballot set to close on November 21, over 32 million match registrations have already been completed, indicating widespread interest beyond just high-value fixtures.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/icc-mens-cricket-world-cup-2027-over-120-million-ticket-requ.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Fans from South Africa, the UK, India, the USA, and Australia are leading the charge in ballot registrations. The African continent, including co-hosts Zimbabwe and Namibia, has generated significant interest, with more than 14.9 million ticket applications (14,936,708). South Africa, which will host 41 fixtures across eight venues, has attracted over 105 million applications, while matches involving the Proteas (11 fixtures, including pre-seeded Super 7 matches) have amassed nearly 26 million ticket requests. Zimbabwe has seen its accumulated ticket requests cross the 13 million mark (13,350,190), with the Zimbabwe vs India clash on October 24 at the new Mosi-oa-Tunya International Cricket Stadium at Victoria Falls tallying over 3.3 million requests. Namibia Cricket Ground in Windhoek has received over two million ticket requests for its six fixtures, nearly 900,000 of which are for the England vs Bangladesh match on October 8.</p><p>High-profile encounters are drawing immense attention, with the October 10 clash between India and Pakistan in Johannesburg attracting more than eight million ticket applications (8,397,294). The Final, scheduled for November 21 in the same city, is also in high demand with nearly eight million requests (7,900,944). Gaurav Saxena, ICC General Manager – Events and Cricket Operations, described the response as &quot;outstanding,&quot; reflecting the tournament's global appeal and its significance as it returns to Africa for the first time in 24 years. He noted that the &quot;strong interest across South Africa, Zimbabwe and Namibia proves the scale of the opportunity to deliver a world-class event.&quot;</p><p>Qondisa Ngwenya, ICC Men’s Cricket World Cup 2027 LOC CEO, echoed this sentiment, calling the demand &quot;extraordinary&quot; and a &quot;powerful indication of the excitement.&quot; Ngwenya highlighted the opportunity to showcase the people, cultures, and hospitality of the three host nations to a global audience. The tournament, featuring 57 matches across 12 venues in October and November 2027, marks Namibia’s first time staging Men’s Cricket World Cup matches and will showcase Zimbabwe’s new stadium at Victoria Falls. The ballot process is free, not first-come, first-served, and allows one entry per person per match, with successful applicants notified by email.</p><p>What to watch: The final surge in ticket ballot registrations before the November 21 deadline.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately reflects the source's data and quotes while maintaining a professional news tone.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/2027-world-cup-fever-soars-as-icc-gets-over-120-million-ticket-requests-in-ballot-3173/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
