     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-11-02 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Indian Navy Rescues 20 Crew from Pirates 3,000 Km from Mumbai</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Indian Navy</span> <span>#Anti-Piracy</span> <span>#Arabian Sea</span> <span>#National Security</span></div>
       <p>The Indian Navy has successfully rescued all 20 crew members of the fishing vessel Azini from armed pirates following a meticulously coordinated 48-hour operation in the Arabian Sea. The vessel was reportedly seized more than 3,000 km from Mumbai, according to the Times of India.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/indian-navy-rescues-20-crew-from-pirates-3000-km-from-mumbai.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Defence Minister Rajnath Singh praised the Indian Navy for its swift action, executed with support from the Indian Air Force. Singh stated that the &quot;swift localisation, interception and safe release of the crew and vessel from significantly-armed pirates, with timely support from the IAF, speak well of the Indian Navy’s high operational readiness and ability to respond decisively to maritime threats across the region.&quot; A photograph shared by the minister on X showed destroyer INS Kolkata standing by as commandos guarded what appeared to be the pirates on the fishing vessel's deck, alongside a large cache of seized weapons.</p><p>Singh further emphasized that &quot;Our brave personnel have once again demonstrated India’s ability, intent and resolve to safeguard lives, uphold maritime security and ensure stability across the Indian Ocean Region.&quot; He added that India &quot;continues to stand resolute in ensuring SAFE SEAS and a free, open and secure IOR.&quot; This operation, launched on a Monday, occurred further out than the Navy's landmark anti-piracy mission in March 2024, when the same destroyer, INS Kolkata, intercepted the pirate ship MV Ruen approximately 2,600km from the Indian coast.</p><p>The Indian Navy maintains a significant presence in the region, deploying over 10 warships for anti-piracy and regional security operations under initiatives like Operation Sankalp. These patrols cover the Arabian Sea, Gulf of Aden, and the East Coast of Africa. Since 2008, the Indian Navy has proactively engaged with regional and extra-regional navies and maritime forces to promote maritime security in these crucial waters through anti-piracy patrols.</p><p>What to watch: Continued Indian Navy operations to secure maritime routes in the Indian Ocean Region.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report is comprehensive, accurate, and effectively synthesizes the provided source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/navy-rescues-20-crew-members-of-fishing-vessel-from-armed-pirates-over-3000km-from-mumbai-rajnath-praises-48-hr-op/articleshow/135473804.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/mumbai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mumbai</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
