     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-11-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India's Emissions Growth Slows in 2025, UNEP Report Notes</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#emissions</span> <span>#UNEP</span> <span>#climate change</span> <span>#renewable energy</span></div>
       <p>India's upward trend of greenhouse gas emissions showed a significant change in 2025, pivoting from a path indicating rapid growth to a slight decline. This finding is reported in the United Nations Environment Programme's (UNEP) new &quot;Emissions Gap Report 2026: The Point of Return,&quot; released on October 21. The report draws on data from multiple sources, with the Emissions Database for Global Atmospheric Research (EDGAR) and the International Energy Agency (IEA) both indicating a slight decline in India's emissions, while the Energy Institute estimates a marginal increase. Crucially, all three sources point to a departure from the country's previous pattern of swift emissions expansion.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/indias-emissions-growth-slows-in-2025-unep-report-notes.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The report attributes this shift partly to weather effects and structural changes within India's energy landscape, specifically towards renewable energy. Minal Pathak, a researcher at Ahmedabad University and a lead author of the UNEP report’s chapter on global emissions trends, commented in a written response to Mongabay-India that &quot;The data this year is very encouraging.&quot; She further clarified that these changes are partly due to weather effects and structural shifts towards renewable energy, as highlighted in Chapter 2 of the report.</p><p>Despite this positive development, India remains the world’s third-largest greenhouse gas emitter, following China and the United States. The country's emissions reached approximately 4.5 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide equivalent (CO₂e) in 2025, a figure that excludes land use and forestry. However, India's per capita emissions stood at 3.1 tonnes, which is less than half the global average of 6.4 tonnes, providing a comparative perspective on its environmental impact.</p><p>What to watch: Continued monitoring of renewable energy's impact on India's emissions trajectory.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article is comprehensive, though it leaves out the specific mention of the CREA analysis mentioned at the very end of the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/2026/11/indias-emissions-growth-slows-as-renewable-energy-begins-replacing-coal-unep-report-finds/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
