     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-02 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>India's Tax Filings in 2026 Reveal Diverse Incomes, Complex Returns</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#income tax</span> <span>#filing</span> <span>#investments</span> <span>#freelancing</span></div>
       <p>India's income tax filing data for assessment year (AY) 2026-27, as detailed in ClearTax's annual report &quot;How India Filed in 2026,&quot; reveals significant shifts in financial habits. A notable 47.3% of self-filers reported income from at least one non-salary source, a substantial increase from just 9.5% in AY 2022-23. Furthermore, the proportion of taxpayers reporting income from two or more non-salary sources surged from 3.5% to 24.4% over the same period, indicating a growing diversification beyond traditional employment. These additional income streams include investments, business ventures, freelancing, and even cryptocurrency, reflecting a broader financial landscape for many Indians.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/indias-tax-filings-in-2026-reveal-diverse-incomes-complex-re.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Younger taxpayers are increasingly navigating complex financial scenarios. Among self-filers below 25, 75% filed complex returns (ITR-2, ITR-3, or ITR-4) in AY 2026-27, up from 60% in the preceding assessment year. This shift was particularly evident in ITR-3 filings, which jumped from 28% to 44% for this age group, while ITR-1 filings declined from 41% to 25%. The ClearTax report links this trend to activities such as stock market trading, freelancing, and small business ventures among younger taxpayers. Surprisingly, even low-income earners are filing complex returns, with 73% of taxpayers reporting gross income below ₹1 lakh opting for complex forms in AY 2026-27, a higher proportion than in income brackets up to ₹50 lakh.</p><p>The data also highlights trends in investment behavior across age groups. Young investors are more frequently reporting capital losses; 37% of self-filers under 25 who reported capital gains or losses in AY 2026-27 reported a loss, the highest among all age groups. This figure decreased with age, reaching 21% for those over 60. Despite these losses, young investors continue to engage with the market, as 82% of first-time filers using ClearTax's expert-assisted plans filed complex returns in AY 2026-27, up from 68% in AY 2024-25. Retirement does not halt investment activity either, with 40% of taxpayers above 60 reporting capital gains in AY 2026-27, compared to 35% among those aged 40-50, and over half of retirees filing complex returns.</p><p>What to watch: The continued evolution of income sources and investment patterns in future tax filings.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the source material, capturing the key data points and the five identified trends accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/income-tax-returns-how-india-filed-in-2026-5-trends-that-reveal-how-young-investors-salaried-earners-manage-money-11793632937864.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
