     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-02 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>ITR Deadline Extended to November 21 for Audit Cases</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Income Tax</span> <span>#ITR</span> <span>#Deadline Extension</span> <span>#CBDT</span></div>
       <p>The Central Board of Direct Taxes (CBDT) has extended the income tax return (ITR) filing deadline for Assessment Year (AY) 2026-27 from October 31 to November 21, 2026. This extension, announced in September, applies to companies and other eligible taxpayers whose accounts are required to be audited under the Income-tax Act. Partners of firms whose accounts are also subject to audit are covered, subject to applicable conditions. The move grants businesses, professionals, and other eligible taxpayers an additional 21 days to complete their tax compliance requirements.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/itr-deadline-extended-to-november-21-for-audit-cases.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This revised deadline is expected to provide relief to taxpayers grappling with multiple compliance obligations. It is particularly beneficial for those needing extra time to complete audits, reconcile financial records, and verify tax-related information before submitting their returns. The extension aims to help businesses finalize audit processes and ensure that financial information reported in ITRs is accurate and supported by appropriate documentation. Tax experts noted that the 21-day extension offers &quot;much-needed breathing space&quot; for businesses and professionals managing complex compliance needs, potentially reducing errors from rushed filings.</p><p>Taxpayers frequently need to reconcile their books of accounts with financial statements, tax audit reports, and other statutory filings before ITR submission. The additional time allows companies to address discrepancies, review tax positions, and complete pending documentation. For audit-covered taxpayers, ITR completion involves more than just reporting income and expenses; it necessitates ensuring consistency across financial statements, tax audit disclosures, and supporting records with the Income Tax Department's requirements. This extension provides an opportunity to review these details carefully and resolve potential discrepancies prior to filing, especially benefiting businesses with complex transactions or multiple reporting obligations.</p><p>Despite the extension, eligible taxpayers should view November 21 as additional compliance time rather than an opportunity to delay filing. Businesses must prioritize pending audits, complete necessary reconciliations, and review their tax positions well before the new deadline. It is also crucial that self-assessment tax liabilities are paid before filing returns to avoid applicable interest under Sections 234A and 234B of the Income-tax Act. Importantly, this extension does not apply to all taxpayers; individuals whose regular ITR filing deadline has expired should confirm their eligibility.</p><p>What to watch: Taxpayers to ensure timely payment of self-assessment tax.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately captures all essential details from the source, including the specific dates, legal context, and the rationale for the extension.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/itr-deadline-pushed-to-november-21-for-audit-cases-what-it-means-for-businesses-and-taxpayers-11793619092158.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
