     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Laser pulses enable precision slicing of lab-grown diamonds</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers from Chiba University have developed a strategy to slice lab-grown diamonds using ultrashort laser pulses. By inducing internal structural modifications, focused lasers create stresses that guide crack propagation, offering an alternative to mechanical machining for high-performance electronics.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/laser-pulses-enable-precision-slicing-of-lab-grown-diamonds.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study, published in Diamond and Related Materials, compared high-pressure high-temperature (HPHT) and chemical vapor deposition (CVD) diamonds. Findings reveal that CVD diamonds respond to shorter pulses than HPHT variants. The team suggests that microscopic defects stemming from specific growth histories, rather than just nitrogen content, significantly influence how these crystals react to laser treatment.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Strong summary of the study findings and the technical context of diamond slicing.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-ultrashort-laser-pulses-slice-lab.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
