     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Magnetic Nanocomposites Enable Smaller Power Components for Computer Chips</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>University of Florida researchers have developed tiny magnetic devices called stripline microinductors to improve power delivery in computer chips. By utilizing a composite of nickel and iron oxide nanoparticles, the team achieved more than twice the inductance of comparable non-magnetic devices, facilitating smaller voltage regulators.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/magnetic-nanocomposites-enable-smaller-power-components-for-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Shrinking inductors is traditionally difficult because smaller dimensions reduce inductance. This new approach uses electroinfiltration to embed nanoparticles within a metal matrix, creating a material that functions effectively at high frequencies. This advancement allows for more compact hardware capable of supplying steady, controlled voltage to transistors in modern semiconductor manufacturing.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively distills the technical process and the performance benefits of the University of Florida's microinductor research.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-magnetic-nanocomposites-components-power-chips.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
