     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-02 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Research Explains Why Birds Flap to Land Despite Energy Cost</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Birds</span> <span>#Flight Physics</span> <span>#Landing</span> <span>#Aerodynamics</span></div>
       <p>Birds may not always follow the optimal physics-suggested flight path for landing, which would involve the safest, lowest possible speed, and least energy, according to new research from The University of Manchester. The study, published in *Proceedings of the Royal Society A Mathematical Physical and Engineering Science*, utilized mathematical modeling to investigate why most birds flap their wings during landing, despite powered flight consuming more energy than gliding.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/new-research-explains-why-birds-flap-to-land-despite-energy-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The research found that a bird flying without flapping could theoretically achieve zero speed at touchdown. However, this maneuver would necessitate significantly higher approach speeds and considerably more space than birds typically use for landing. For instance, a pigeon-sized bird would need to start at more than twice its usual cruising speed, cover approximately 60 meters horizontally, and ascend about 20 meters to perform such an unpowered, zero-speed landing.</p><p>These findings suggest that attaining the absolute lowest touchdown speed is often impractical in real-world landing environments. Birds likely use flapping not just to decelerate, but to do so effectively within the confined spaces available. Lead author Dr. Ben Parslew, a lecturer in aerospace engineering at The University of Manchester, noted that landing presents a &quot;fundamental physical challenge&quot; for birds, requiring them to reduce speed while maintaining aerodynamic control.</p><p>Additionally, the modeling offers new insights into the swooping behaviors observed in some perching birds. While previous research on Harris's hawks suggested swooping minimized distance traveled after wing stall, the new model indicates this behavior reflects a balance between achieving a low touchdown speed and limiting the time spent in a stalled condition, with touchdown speed having a greater influence than previously documented.</p><p>Landing on the ground presents a different set of challenges compared to perching, as birds cannot continue their trajectory below the surface or climb through their destination. The modeling revealed that a bird landing on the ground without flapping cannot entirely eliminate its speed before contact, necessitating the use of its wings, legs, or both to dissipate the remaining energy. This imposes physical demands on the bird's body, requiring more robust muscles and bones, particularly in the legs, which in turn adds mass and can reduce flight performance.</p><p>The researchers propose that this inherent trade-off—between being light enough for efficient flight and robust enough for safe landings—may have played a significant role in shaping bird evolution. The same physical principles could also help explain how other flying animals manage unpowered landings.</p><p>What to watch: Further research into the evolutionary implications of landing physics on avian species.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the research findings regarding bird landing physics and the evolutionary trade-offs discussed in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-birds-physics.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
