     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Procrastination impacts over 70% of students</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A recent U.S. study involving 65,000 college students found that procrastination and stress are the most prevalent issues affecting academic performance. Procrastination, defined as an unnecessary and voluntary delay, is frequently linked to self-regulation failures rather than a simple lack of willpower.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/procrastination-impacts-over-70-of-students.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Students aged 14 to 29, those with ADHD, or individuals facing challenging tasks are most susceptible to chronic procrastination. While some students believe they perform better under pressure, researchers note that delaying tasks often increases long-term stress and guilt. Experts suggest that overcoming this cycle requires addressing confidence gaps and improving self-regulation skills to better manage academic goals and deadlines.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the research findings and the link between self-regulation and academic performance.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-students-procrastinate-stay-track.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
