     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-02 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Proximity to Green Space Linked to Improved Attention in Children</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A study from Washington University in St. Louis suggests that access to nature can mitigate &quot;directed attention fatigue&quot; in children. Analyzing data from the ABCD study involving 12,000 children, researchers found that greater proximity to parks and woods correlates with improved performance on objective assessments of sustained attention and inhibitory control.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/proximity-to-green-space-linked-to-improved-attention-in-chi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Published in Environment International, the research accounted for variables such as income, ADHD diagnoses, and safety perceptions. Psychologists emphasize that the restorative effects of green space are contingent on feelings of safety and reduced social media use while outdoors. These findings support attention restoration theory, suggesting that natural environments offer measurable cognitive benefits for youth.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately distills the study's focus on green space and its correlation with cognitive benefits.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-attention-nature.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
