     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Six Indian tourists rescued from Nepal's Shey-Phoksundo National Park</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Six Indian tourists, two women and four men, were rescued by a private helicopter on Monday after being stranded by heavy snowfall in Nepal's Shey-Phoksundo National Park. The group had been out of contact since November 1 and were trapped at Numla and Wagla pass despite warnings from local authorities and their guide.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/six-indian-tourists-rescued-from-nepals-shey-phoksundo-natio.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The tourists are currently receiving treatment at a government hospital in the Dune of Dolpa district and are in stable condition. Officials noted the group had entered the area without required administrative permits. Previous attempts to rescue the hikers on Sunday were aborted due to adverse weather conditions.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the rescue operation and the status of the tourists without adding filler.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/six-indian-tourists-stranded-in-nepals-national-park-rescued-safely/article71655445.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
