     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-02 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study details 17th-century Transylvanian climate via historical records</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Researchers from the University of Oradea analyzed 937 historical accounts to document climate extremes in 17th-century Transylvania. The study, published in PLOS Climate, cross-referenced town records, church logs, and diaries with physical evidence like tree rings to confirm 55 exceptionally harsh winters.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-02/study-details-17th-century-transylvanian-climate-via-histori.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The team identified that 36 of these severe winters occurred between 1645 and 1700, a period marked by the Little Ice Age. Beyond cold, records indicate frequent excessive rainfall contributed to crop failures, skyrocketing food prices, and disease outbreaks. These environmental stressors exacerbated the daily struggles of populations already facing war and political instability.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately synthesizes the climate study data and its social-historical context.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-historical-reveal-bitter-truth-17th.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
